---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import FailClosedSecurityGate from "../components/diagrams/FailClosedSecurityGate.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security | BeaconGuard Fail-Closed Authorization Boundaries"
  description="BeaconGuard applies fail-closed authorization at request admission and declared enterprise actions, with controls against forgery, replay, misbinding, bypass, evidence failure, and verification privilege escalation."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Security for governed AI transactions</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      BeaconGuard authorizes whether an enterprise application may invoke an AI
      provider and whether a declared enterprise action may proceed. Runtime
      outcomes are ALLOW or DENY only. Failures return DENY. Evidence supports
      investigation; it does not replace SIEM, IAM, GRC, or customer security
      programs.
    </p>
    <p>
      No AI-generated result becomes authorized merely because the provider
      returned it successfully.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Two authorization boundaries</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Request Admission Authorization</strong> decides whether the
      application may invoke the AI provider. <strong>Result-to-Action
      Authorization</strong> decides whether the declared intended enterprise
      action may proceed. The AI-generated result remains an untrusted proposal.
      Independent action authorization is not semantic response-content scoring.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Fail-closed security gate</h2>
    <p>
      When a required trust, replay, authorization, release, or evidence
      dependency is unavailable or cannot be verified, the governed operation
      does not fall back to permissive execution. BeaconGuard returns DENY and
      prevents provider invocation or enterprise effect as applicable. There is
      no review-as-permission fallback.
    </p>
    <FailClosedSecurityGate />
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Threat coverage</h2>
    <p>
      Controls are designed to address the following classes of risk across
      request admission, result handling, action authorization, and
      verification. Exact controls depend on configuration and deployment.
      Tenant isolation claims reflect lab-validated controls with residual
      customer-live risk.
    </p>

    <h3>Request admission</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Request forgery</strong> — authenticated request context is
        required before governed evaluation; missing or invalid trust material
        fails closed as DENY.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Replay</strong> — freshness and replay resistance apply where
        shared operation is configured; previously consumed authority fails
        closed rather than being accepted by another instance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cross-tenant request use</strong> — authorization context is
        bound to tenant and environment; authority from one boundary is not
        silently accepted in another.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Provider result handling</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Provider-response substitution</strong> — a returned result does
        not become enterprise authority; substitution cannot mint ALLOW for a
        declared action without independent authorization.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Result-to-request misbinding</strong> — declared actions must be
        authorized in governed transaction context; a result cannot silently
        inherit authority from an unrelated request.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Wrong-tenant or wrong-environment result use</strong> — tenant
        and environment binding constrains where a governed result may be used
        for a later declared action.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Result reuse</strong> — reuse of a prior AI-generated result as
        standing permission is not a supported authorization path; later effect
        requires independent authorization of the declared action.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Malformed structured output</strong> — malformed or incomplete
        structured proposals do not bypass authorization; invalid context fails
        closed rather than permitting enterprise effect.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Declared action authorization</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Action-contract substitution</strong> — authorization applies to
        the declared intended enterprise action under the Active Verified
        Release; substituting a different action contract requires a new
        governed authorization.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Proposed-action replay</strong> — proposed or previously
        considered actions do not create reusable standing permission; single-use
        and freshness controls apply where configured.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Unauthorized action expansion</strong> — ALLOW for one declared
        action does not expand into broader or different enterprise effects
        without additional authorization.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Provider-to-application bypass</strong> — a successful provider
        response does not authorize enterprise execution by itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Result-to-Action authorization bypass</strong> — enterprise effect must
        pass Result-to-Action Authorization where configured; the untrusted
        proposal path cannot skip that boundary by treating provider success as
        ALLOW.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Evidence failure before enterprise effect</strong> — when
        required evidence persistence cannot complete on the action path,
        unauthorized or unverifiable effect is hard-stopped rather than allowed
        to proceed without a decision record.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Verification surface</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Verification privilege escalation</strong> — BeaconGuard Verify
        is read-only; investigation access cannot grant runtime authority,
        activate a release, alter a Compliance Playbook, or authorize an
        enterprise action.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cross-tenant report access</strong> — reporting and exploration
        are subject to tenant isolation controls; investigation views are not a
        path to cross-tenant runtime authority.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Export alteration</strong> — verifiable export supports integrity
        checks for investigation packages; browser-side claims are untrusted for
        integrity conclusions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI-insight contamination</strong> — AI-Assisted Insights are
        non-authoritative and remain outside the trusted reconstruction and
        runtime authorization path.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Request authenticity and trust checks</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard requires authenticated request context before governed
      evaluation. Required trust signals and integrity expectations must be
      satisfied. Missing, invalid, mismatched, or out-of-bounds trust material
      fails closed as DENY.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Tenant and environment isolation</h2>
    <p>
      Authorization context is bound to the applicable tenant and environment.
      Lab-validated tenant isolation controls apply; residual risk remains for
      customer-live deployments and shared infrastructure choices.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Release verification and activation</h2>
    <p>
      Only an Active Verified Release governs runtime evaluation. Distributed
      releases must pass Release Verification and Activation before use.
      Mix-and-match or unverified artifacts are outside the approved path.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Bounded review for a new governed request</h2>
    <p>
      A privileged review may authorize a <em>new</em>, bounded request after
      step-up verification. It does not change the original DENY, resume the
      original request or provider response, convert the same decision to
      ALLOW, or create reusable standing permission.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>External dependencies and customer responsibility</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Identity providers and source-system trust remain customer-controlled.</li>
      <li>Network, TLS, secrets, and host hardening remain deployment responsibilities.</li>
      <li>
        Replay-state providers, when used, are external to core decision
        evaluation and must be configured for fail-closed operation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Evidence storage durability and integrity protection depend on the
        configured evidence provider and customer infrastructure.
      </li>
      <li>Operational monitoring and incident response remain shared customer responsibilities.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>What BeaconGuard is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Not a replacement for SIEM, IAM, GRC, WAF, or API gateway platforms</li>
      <li>Not a probabilistic authorization engine or response-content scorer</li>
      <li>Not a certification of clinical correctness, legal compliance, or legal admissibility</li>
      <li>Not claimed here as customer-production authorized or third-party certified</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/proof-evidence">Review Proof and Evidence</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/verification">Explore Verification</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/trust-assurance">Trust Center</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/docs/threat-model">Threat Model</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
